      },
      "text": "Theme | Core sub-themes | Illustrative quote | Meaning for operations\nFragmented recordkeeping creates no single source of truth | Manual and hybrid recording; person-dependent updates; informal workarounds. | \"So far, we do not have a system for tracking our sales and inventory...\" (Interviewee 1) | Operations depend on scattered records rather than a unified live record.\nDelayed updates make the accurate picture arrive too late | Unposted movement; next-day correction; decisions made under uncertainty. | \"The deeper issue is that the real situation becomes visible too late.\" (Interviewee 3) | Even when the truth is eventually reconstructed, it often arrives after the decision window.\nInaccurate records disrupt service, fulfillment, and restocking | Customer promise failure; partial release; conservative or delayed restocking. | \"This can disappoint customers and reduce sales.\" (Interviewee 2) | Weak records quickly become visible as stockouts, delays, and missed sales.\nManual reconciliation shifts effort from operations to error correction | Repeated recounting; after-hours verification; coordination strain. | \"Sometimes it ends up in an argument because I am already loaded with work.\" (Interviewee 1) | Labor is redirected from serving customers and planning toward repetitive correction.\nWhen records conflict, trust shifts to physical proof | Physical count as truth; signed slips and receipts preferred; slow confirmation. | \"We usually check the actual physical stock first because that is the most reliable basis.\" (Interviewee 2) | Record systems have not earned enough trust to support fast decisions on their own.",
